LOGBOOK.md:2115:- CORRECTION (2026-09-02, revision 4): the sentence above originally read "all 135 bound arguments and refusal guards in `core_records.go`" and "All now covered". That does not reproduce and overstated the sweep. What that sweep enumerated was every *bound argument* plus the guards whose condition it could rewrite to `false`; it was not the refusal-guard set. A statement-coverage profile of the same tree shows **91 runtime refusal branches in `core_records.go` that no test executed once**, at 87.7% package coverage, and the round-3 review deleted eight normative gates simultaneously with `go test ./...` still fully green. The correct claim for revision 3 is: a derived sweep over bound arguments found 8 survivors among the mutants it generated, and said nothing about the refusal-guard set.
LOGBOOK.md:2134:- FINDING: A derived refusal inventory can prove a site was reached. It cannot prove the site decided the outcome. Those are different facts, and positive-path-only coverage of a refusal looks identical to real coverage in both the inventory and the mutant count until the fixture is built so that every preceding check passes.
LOGBOOK.md:2136:- ANOMALY: The blob-leaf regularity clause bounds availability, not only safety. `os.Open` and SQLite block indefinitely on a FIFO with no writer, so removing the clause converts a refusal into an unbounded hang inside `OpenProjection`. A negative case that calls the entry point inline would hang the suite instead of reddening it; the case must assert a bounded return. Mutant `m11` reddens after 20.04s at that bound.
